# AGENTS.md

Operating manual for coding agents working in this repository: rules and traps, not history.
What was done when, and the measurements behind each decision, live in `docs/`. If you are
about to add a dated narrative here, it belongs in `docs/HISTORY.md`.

## What this repository is

Not a software project: no build, lint or test suite. It is a LitTrans translation
workspace — the project state, decisions and evidence for translating one document. Every
tracked file is a decision that later batch runs and audits depend on; changing one can
invalidate work that already passed review.

| File | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `README.md` | Handbook: the document, page table, how to run it, current status |
| `docs/LITTRANS.md` | What the installed plugin guarantees (generated; never hand-edited) |
| `docs/HISTORY.md` | Dated record of what was done |
| `docs/DECISIONS.md` | Rulings, rejected alternatives, lessons |
| `docs/TERMINOLOGY.md` | Gate design rules, per-chapter promotion procedure, measured rejections |
| `docs/REVIEWS.md` | Persistent copy of each chapter's review ledger |
| `PLUGIN-ISSUES.md` | Cumulative plugin defect ledger with a status index |

## Running anything: use the launcher

`tools/lt.cmd <command>` (Windows) or `tools/lt.sh <command>` (POSIX) — never invoke the
plugin's `littrans.py` directly. `tools/lt.cmd doctor` is the runtime self-check. After a
plugin upgrade run `tools/lt.cmd project scaffold {{ project_rel }} --refresh` so
`docs/LITTRANS.md` describes the installed build.

## Hard constraints

- **Never add a public remote.** The record embeds excerpts of a copyrighted source.
- **The source PDF never enters git; the extraction and its decision trace do.**
  `tools/lt.cmd project tracked {{ project_rel }}` is the authority — it asks git.
- **Write LF, never CRLF.** Pass `newline="\n"` from Python; pin `eol=lf` in `.gitattributes`.
- **Always pass `--pages`.** It defaults to the whole document.
- **One host writes at a time.** Pull before writing, run `project tracked` before
  committing, push after; a conflict in a record file means both hosts wrote — take one side
  whole, never merge JSON or JSONL by hand. Every host runs the same plugin build (`doctor`
  → `build`); the PDF sits at `source/` on each host and never in git.

## Workflow

Stages are sequential and each has its own review state; source coverage, translation
approval and structured-asset correctness are independent. The stage names, wave limits
and reviewer batch limits are in `docs/LITTRANS.md`. Record here the project-specific
traps (where chapters end, what the extractor gets wrong for this document).

## Dispatching writers and reviewers

The packet's `model` is a dispatch category, not a claim about the served model, and a packet
may record none: that dispatch runs on the host's own default. Reviewer lenses run as fresh,
mutually blind tasks and are read-only; the parent imports their findings. Record here the
host-specific dispatch rules this project uses.

## Editing terminology

Three stores, and the difference is what the audit hash can see:

| Store | Effect | In the audit hash |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `{{ project_prefix }}glossary/approved.yaml` | hard gate, per unit | only entries matching the packet's units |
| `{{ project_prefix }}glossary/reference.yaml` | binding, not a gate (`kind` groups: proper names, senses, ...) | only entries matching the packet's units |
| `{{ project_prefix }}context/*.md` (brief, style guide) | injected verbatim | whole file |

Before editing a context file ask whether a rule changed or a record was added — a record
goes to `reference.yaml`. `candidates.yaml` gates nothing; it records promotion decisions.

## Audit staleness and revision cost

Editing a context file marks every existing audit `context-changed`; editing a term resets
only the batches that mention it. `workflow status` names the changed part and its growth.
Freeze the brief and style guide before a chapter's audit wave.

## Logging plugin defects

Append to `PLUGIN-ISSUES.md`: symbol name plus reproduction plus observed result, never a
bare line number (line numbers move between builds).
